II. WHAT THIS PROFESSION ACTUALLY DOES — AUGMENTED DEFINITION

The Autonomous Habitat Architect designs the integrated physical and computational architecture of a lunar habitat that can assess its own state, predict its own failure trajectories, prioritize its own repair needs, and execute maintenance operations with minimal human intervention across a multi-decade operational life — while dynamically co-scheduling human and robotic presence to prevent mutual interference, and while operating under strict energy and thermal computing budgets.

The four core functions remain — self-assessment, failure prediction, repair prioritization, autonomous execution — but each now carries hardened sub-requirements:

Self-assessment must occur on a sensor fabric that extracts features at the edge, transmitting compact metadata rather than raw waveform floods, and must cross-validate measurements across physically independent sensing modalities that are themselves immune to single-event radiation corruption.

Failure prediction must run on a Prognostic Architecture whose long-horizon warnings are gated by actionable criticality — invisible to the crew until intervention is genuinely mandatory — and whose underlying material state model incorporates the entire history of autonomous repairs as an evolving structural biography, not a static birth certificate.

Repair prioritization must respect a formal hierarchy that treats not only life-safety and mission-criticality but also the material compatibility of any proposed repair with the local thermal expansion environment, the current harmonic interference risk from human activity, and the remaining useful life of the element relative to the time required to complete the repair.

Autonomous execution must unfold inside dynamically computed 4D spacetime safety envelopes that prevent maintenance robots and human occupants from imposing fatal vibratory or chemical loads on curing repair materials, while ensuring that the repair itself is permanently recorded in an immutable structural mutation ledger so that future diagnostic models do not misinterpret a patched, evolved structure as the original pristine design.

These expansions do not make the profession more complex for its own sake. They make it survivable.

