IV. FAILURE MODE 1 — SENSOR MONOCULTURE AND THE LAYERED SENSING ARCHITECTURE (HARDENED)

Original problem statement (v1.0) retained. Single-technology sensing systems fail through shared mechanisms and produce correlated false readings. The lunar environment amplifies this risk. The Layered Sensing Architecture — three physically independent modalities per life-critical parameter — remains the mandatory solution.

v2.0 Hardening: Edge-Compute Pruning Mandate

The original architecture generates a continuous deluge of multi-modal raw data — high-frequency acoustic emission waveforms, full-spectrum optical interferometry streams, distributed fiber Bragg grating strain fields refreshed at kilohertz rates. Transmitting and storing this unstructured torrent on the habitat’s limited edge-computing and data-logging infrastructure (typically radiation-hardened 16GB-class nodes) would choke the diagnostic pipeline within hours. Worse, it would force the Cross-System Coupling Model to burn computational and thermal budget on ingesting noise.

Mandatory Addition to the Layered Sensing Specification:
Every sensor node must perform In-Situ Feature Extraction before transmitting data upstream. The physical sensor housing must contain a radiation-hardened edge processor that converts raw waveforms into compact, high-entropy metadata packets — transmitting peak frequencies, drift deltas, energy-release counts, and anomaly flag vectors, not the full time-series stream. This reduces per-sensor data volume by two to three orders of magnitude while preserving the diagnostic information required for failure detection and trend monitoring.

Design implications:

· Sensor selection is now a joint hardware-firmware decision; the Autonomous Habitat Architect must specify the edge feature set alongside the physical sensing modality.
· The cross-validation logic in the Layered Sensing Architecture operates on feature vectors, not raw signals, and must be validated against information loss thresholds.
· The compute-thermodynamic governor (Section IX, Layer 1) monitors the aggregate power draw of edge processing and may scale feature extraction depth under thermal duress.

Updated Metric for Success:
Zero correlated multi-sensor false negatives. Cross-modality agreement >99.5%. Edge-processed data throughput per critical parameter <2% of raw-equivalent bandwidth. No diagnostic pipeline saturation events during continuous operations.

