V. FAILURE MODE 2 — MATERIAL AMNESIA, THE LIVING MATERIAL STATE MODEL, AND THE STRUCTURAL MUTATION LEDGER

Original problem statement (v1.0) retained. A diagnostic AI calibrated against Day 1 material properties becomes increasingly obsolete as lunar aging diverges from terrestrial predictions. The Living Material State Model — continuously updated via embedded non-destructive evaluation — remains the core solution.

v2.0 Hardening 1: The Material Compatibility Matrix for Autonomous Repair

Autonomous repair actions — extrusion of regolith-based pastes, injection of polymer sealants, thermal curing of self-healing agents — deposit new material onto aged parent structures. On the Moon, the 260°C day-night thermal swing creates catastrophic interfacial stresses if the repair material’s coefficient of thermal expansion (CTE) does not match the parent structure within a defined tolerance band. A mismatched patch will peel, crack, or explosively delaminate within one to five diurnal cycles.

Mandatory Addition to Layer 1 (Material and Structural Architecture):
The Interfacial Shear and CTE Management Protocol must be specified for every class of autonomous repair material. Before any repair action is authorized, the autonomous system queries the Living Material State Model for the current local CTE of the parent material (accounting for its age, radiation dose, and prior repair history) and selects a repair formulation whose CTE matches within a critical tolerance Δα ≤ 5×10⁻⁷ /°C at the bond interface. If no compatible repair material is available in local inventory, the repair is flagged for human decision, and the Prognostic Architecture accelerates monitoring of the unrepaired defect.

v2.0 Hardening 2: The Structural Mutation Ledger

A self-repairing habitat operating for 20 years will continuously alter its own walls, replacing and augmenting material until the original Earth-validated geometry and material map no longer correspond to physical reality. If the Living Material State Model does not track the cumulative history of every repair — what was added, where, when, with what chemistry — then the diagnostic AI will gradually lose the ability to distinguish a repaired section from a pristine one, leading to blind spots where old repairs interact with new stresses in unmodeled ways. This is the “Ship of Theseus” epistemic trap.

Mandatory Addition to Layer 3 (Operational Integration):
Every autonomous repair action must generate a permanent, immutable block in the Structural Mutation Ledger (SML) — a local, cryptographically chained record containing:

· Sector_ID: precise spatial coordinates of the modification.
· Material_Ancestry: exact batch number and chemical/rheological formulation of the extruded or injected material.
· Interfacial_CTE: measured Coefficient of Thermal Expansion at the patch-parent boundary.
· Sensing_Continuity_Status: verification of whether embedded fiber Bragg gratings or acoustic emission conduits were severed or successfully bridged during the repair.
· Timestamp: mission elapsed time and lunar thermal phase at the moment of application.

The Living Material State Model must ingest the SML as a primary data source, dynamically updating the material properties, geometry, and sensor topology of the affected sector. No diagnostic assessment of a repaired sector may be generated without referencing the SML entries for that location. The SML is the habitat’s memory of its own physical evolution — without it, the habitat is amnesic by design.

Updated Metric for Success:
Living Material State Model property prediction error <5% at 2-year assessment, <8% at 20-year assessment. Zero instances of repair delamination attributable to CTE mismatch. Structural ancestry traceable without gaps for 100% of autonomously modified surface area.

