VI. FAILURE MODE 3 — EXPANDED COUPLING: METABOLIC, GEOTECHNICAL, AND COMPUTATIONAL

Original problem statement (v1.0) retained. Domain-isolated diagnostics miss failure cascades that propagate across structural, thermal, atmospheric, and seal boundaries. The Cross-System Coupling Model — a unified physics-based simulation of the habitat as a single coupled system — remains the mandatory solution.

v2.0 Expansion 1: Metabolic Human-Structure Coupling

The original Cross-System Coupling Model treated the human crew as a protected payload, not as a dynamic physical input. This is a critical omission. Human occupants are localized, time-varying sources of moisture, CO₂, thermal energy, and mechanical vibration (footfalls, exercise equipment, door operations). These outputs are not negligible boundary conditions; they drive internal condensation cycles that can leach regolith composites, accelerate corrosion at metallic interfaces, and impose cyclic fatigue loads on floor structures that the static structural model never anticipated.

Mandatory Integration:
The Cross-System Coupling Model must incorporate a real-time Human State Vector — a continuously updated map of crew location, activity type, metabolic output, and imposed vibratory spectrum — and couple it to:

· The local humidity and condensation risk model.
· The thermal bridge analysis (body heat and equipment heat).
· The structural fatigue accumulation model (dynamic loading from human movement).
· The acoustic emission network (to differentiate human-induced noise from genuine microcrack signals).

A structural strain reading that coincides with a scheduled crew exercise session in the adjacent module carries a different diagnostic meaning than the same reading during a quiet sleep period. The coupling model must distinguish them, or it will generate false positives and false negatives.

v2.0 Expansion 2: Geotechnical-Substrate Coupling

A lunar habitat is not an island. It is physically and thermally welded to the lunar crust. The original blueprint omitted foundation-level interactions. Two mechanisms demand inclusion:

· Long-Duration Moonquakes: The lunar crust lacks liquid water to attenuate seismic waves. Deep moonquakes and thermal moonquakes can sustain structural vibrations for up to 60 minutes, imposing fatigue cycles that terrestrial structural models do not anticipate. The Living Material State Model must process seismic accelerometer inputs from foundation-level sensors to recalculate fatigue accumulation in real time.
· Cryo-Settling: During the 14-day lunar night, extreme thermal contraction of the upper regolith layers shifts the loading vectors on foundation elements. The Cross-System Coupling Model must dynamically alter foundation sensor baselines to distinguish seasonal ground movement from genuine structural settlement or failure.

Mandatory Addition:
A Foundation Health Monitoring Subsystem integrated into the Cross-System Coupling Model, with dedicated accelerometers and distributed strain sensors at all foundation-regolith interfaces, feeding both the Living Material State Model and the geotechnical coupling module. The model must predict differential settlement trajectories and flag developing foundation anomalies on the same prognostic timeline as atmospheric and structural threats.

v2.0 Expansion 3: Compute-Thermodynamic Governor (CTG)

The Cross-System Coupling Model’s high-fidelity finite element and fluid dynamics computations consume power and reject heat. On the Moon, both are scarce. If the model operates at maximum fidelity during a thermal peak (lunar noon), it can starve life support of cooling capacity. If it is naively throttled, diagnostic accuracy degrades. The solution is a hardwired governor that ties computational depth to the habitat’s instantaneous power and thermal budgets.

Mandatory Addition to Layer 1 (Material and Structural Architecture):
The Compute-Thermodynamic Governor (CTG) is a hardware-software constraint loop that dynamically scales simulation fidelity F(t) according to:

F(t) = F_max * min( P(t)/P_nominal , (Q_rej_max - Q_rej(t))/Q_rej_margin )

where P(t) is available power, Q_rej(t) is current thermal rejection load, and Q_rej_margin is a safety buffer. Under thermal stress, the CTG automatically drops from non-linear continuum models to linear tensor approximations, preserving essential diagnostic coverage without compromising life-support cooling.

Updated Metric for Success (Failure Mode 3):
Zero undetected cross-system cascades beyond early-stage detection. Mean cascade detection time <72 hours from upstream anomaly. Human metabolic and geotechnical couplings active in the model for 100% of occupied hours. No life-support thermal budget violations attributable to computational load.

