VII. FAILURE MODE 4 — COMPETENCE INVISIBILITY, RADIATION-AUDITED AI, AND ACTIVE EPISTEMIC FORAGING

Original problem statement (v1.0) retained. An AI operating outside its training distribution continues to generate confident but incorrect diagnoses. The Boundary Declaration Protocol — continuous self-monitoring of prediction-reality divergence, highest-priority alert, and safe mode — remains the mandatory solution.

v2.0 Hardening 1: Adversarial Radiation Auditing Loop (Triple Modular Redundancy Compute Audit)

Lunar surface radiation — galactic cosmic rays and solar particle events — causes Single Event Upsets (SEUs): bit-flips in memory or logic that can silently corrupt the diagnostic AI’s neural weights, physics-model constants, or inference pathways. A corrupted AI may falsely trigger the Boundary Declaration Protocol, or worse, fail to trigger it when the model genuinely diverges. In the worst case, a radiation strike could make the AI confidently report that a catastrophic failure is nominal.

The original Boundary Declaration Protocol has no defense against this: it monitors prediction-reality divergence, but if the AI’s reality model itself is corrupted, the divergence metric becomes meaningless.

Mandatory Addition to Layer 2 (Computational Protocol Architecture):
The diagnostic and prognostic AI architectures must run on Triple Modular Redundancy (TMR) Compute Audit — three physically isolated, radiation-hardened processing paths that independently compute every inference. A hardware-level voter compares their outputs continuously. If one path diverges (indicating a localized SEU), its output is discarded, the corrupted module is re-flashed from a radiation-hardened golden memory, and the system continues operating on the two remaining paths without interruption.

Crucially, the three paths must not share a common codebase, compilation environment, or fundamental math library. If a floating-point bug or logic omission exists in a shared dependency, all three paths will fail identically, and the TMR voter will see agreement on a wrong answer. This “decoupled safety inference” requirement must be designed into the software architecture from the start.

v2.0 Hardening 2: Active Epistemic Foraging Protocol (AEFP)

The original Boundary Declaration Protocol specified that upon crossing the competence boundary, the autonomous system enters a safe mode: continue monitoring, suspend affected repairs, wait for human expert assessment. This passive posture is inadequate. The system possesses sensor assets — ultrasonic arrays, polarization-capable imagers, multi-spectral scanners — that can actively interrogate the structure to reduce uncertainty before the human arrives. Wasting the waiting period is operationally negligent.

Mandatory Replacement of Safe Mode Specification:
When the Prediction-Reality Divergence threshold is breached, the affected subsystem immediately triggers the Active Epistemic Foraging Protocol (AEFP) :

1. High-Entropy Structural Interrogation:
   · Fires localized ultrasonic pulse-echo arrays through the surrounding regolith-composite matrix to map density changes, delaminations, and crack networks.
   · Shifts optical and infrared diagnostic cameras to cross-polarization modes to detect micro-stresses invisible to standard illumination.
   · Sweeps through alternative sensing modalities not normally active in the affected sector.
2. Data Isolation and Packaging:
      All data gathered during AEFP is explicitly stripped of historical model priors. It is packaged as raw, unmodeled telemetry — clean sensor truths, uncorrupted by the AI’s potentially flawed interpretation.
3. Human-Ready Diagnostic Brief:
      The Boundary Declaration alert now includes, alongside the divergence parameters, the complete AEFP dataset, structured as a prioritized diagnostic brief for the human expert. The brief highlights anomalies, quantifies uncertainty, and explicitly states: “The following is raw sensor data. No AI interpretation has been applied. We do not know what we are looking at.”

Updated Metric for Success:
Zero instances of AI operating outside competence boundaries without triggering Boundary Declaration. Mean time to declaration <4 hours. AEFP initiated within 60 seconds of declaration trigger. Zero undetected SEU-induced AI corruption events (TMR voter divergence detected and corrected before affecting operational outputs).

