VIII. FAILURE MODE 5 — REPAIR MYOPIA, PROGNOSTIC ARCHITECTURE, AND DYNAMIC CRITICALITY GATING

Original problem statement (v1.0) retained. Reactive maintenance addresses terminal symptoms while causal chains continue developing. The Prognostic Architecture — trajectory-based failure prediction on a weeks-to-months horizon — remains the mandatory solution.

v2.0 Hardening: Dynamic Criticality Gating to Prevent Alarm Fatigue

A highly sensitive Prognostic Architecture tracking thousands of parameters across a decades-long mission will generate an overwhelming number of long-horizon, low-confidence “intervention deadlines.” If the human crew and the secondary AI workforce planners see a dashboard perpetually filled with amber warnings for failures that may never materialize or are three months away, they will experience Prognostic Desensitization — alarm fatigue. The critical warning will be ignored, and the very purpose of the Prognostic Architecture will be defeated.

Mandatory Addition to the Repair Prioritization Hierarchy (Layer 2):
Prognostic warnings are classified into three gated visibility tiers:

· Tier 3 — Silent Tracking: Long-horizon trends (estimated time to threshold > 60 days, or confidence < 70%). These are monitored exclusively by the autonomous system. No human-visible alert is generated. The prognostic engine continues refining the trajectory and confidence.
· Tier 2 — Workforce-Aware Scheduling: Mid-horizon trends (30–60 days, confidence > 70%). The repair is added to the autonomous maintenance scheduler’s work queue and the AI Workforce Designer’s resource model, but no crew alert is issued unless the repair requires human authorization per the Decision Topology.
· Tier 1 — Crew-Visible Mandatory Action: Short-horizon or high-consequence trends (estimated remaining useful life < 2× the maximum operational repair time, OR cascade risk exceeds life-critical threshold). A clear, non-optional alert is surfaced to the crew via the Maintenance Status Interface, with a quantified deadline and a pre-computed repair plan.

This gating ensures that human cognitive attention is reserved for situations where human judgment is genuinely mandatory, while the autonomous system absorbs the cognitive load of tracking every developing trend in the background.

Updated Metric for Success:
Preventive-to-reactive maintenance ratio >4:1 by end of first operational year. Zero life-critical failures occurring without prior Tier 1 prognostic alert at least 30 days in advance. Crew-reported alarm fatigue incidents (subjective reporting) near zero. Missed mandatory repair deadlines due to alert invisibility: zero.

