IX. THE THREE DESIGN LAYERS — HARDENED HABITAT EDITION

The five failure modes and their v2.0 hardenings integrate into three design layers as inviolable engineering constraints. These are specified in fabrication sequence — because the Autonomous Habitat Architect’s specifications cannot be added to an existing structure.

Layer 1 — Material and Structural Architecture

(Specified before any habitat component is manufactured)

Mandatory Specifications:

1. Layered Sensing Specification — three physically independent sensing modalities per life-critical parameter, designed into materials during fabrication, with mandatory edge-compute feature extraction at each sensor node. Cross-validation logic specified before sensor selection.
2. Living Material State Model Initialization — degradation models initialized with as-built material properties, including CTE reference values for every structural element. Model starts operating on Day 1 of assembly.
3. Cross-System Coupling Model Architecture — must incorporate, from co-design inception, the metabolic human coupling vectors, the geotechnical-foundation coupling, and the compute-thermodynamic governor (CTG). The CTG must be implemented as a hardwired hardware-software governor, not a software policy that can be disabled.
4. Interfacial Shear and CTE Management Protocol — specified for every autonomous repair material class, with mandatory compatibility verification against the Living Material State Model before any repair authorization.

Layer 2 — Computational and Behavioral Protocol Architecture

(Specified before any autonomous system software is developed)

Mandatory Specifications:

1. Boundary Declaration Protocol (Hardened) — includes the original prediction-reality divergence trigger, the highest-priority signal, and the Active Epistemic Foraging Protocol (AEFP) as the mandatory safe-mode behavior. Passive waiting is forbidden.
2. Triple Modular Redundancy Compute Audit — all diagnostic and prognostic inference paths run on three physically isolated, radiation-hardened compute nodes with decoupled software stacks. Voter logic detects and corrects SEU-induced divergence.
3. Prognostic Architecture with Dynamic Criticality Gating — three-tier visibility gating enforced in the Maintenance Status Interface and the autonomous scheduler. Crew sees only Tier 1 alerts.
4. Repair Prioritization Hierarchy (Hardened) — incorporates the CTE compatibility check, the cascade risk multiplier, and the actionable remaining-useful-life gating as hard constraints. The hierarchy is inviolable.

Layer 3 — Operational Integration Architecture

(Built into colony operational infrastructure)

Mandatory Specifications:

1. Maintenance Status Interface — public, colony-readable display, but filtered through the Dynamic Criticality Gating. Only Tier 1 alerts, Boundary Declaration events, and active repair statuses are surfaced in real time. A deeper diagnostic view is available on-demand but not part of the default ambient display.
2. Human Expert Escalation Protocol (Hardened) — includes the processing of raw AEFP data packages. Specifies which colonists have the competence to analyze unmodeled sensor telemetry for which subsystems.
3. Structural Mutation Ledger (SML) — cryptographically immutable record of every autonomous repair action, ingested by the Living Material State Model as a primary data source. No diagnostic output for a repaired sector may bypass the SML.
4. Dynamic Kinematic Co-Occupation & Safety Protocol (KCSP) — the habitat’s operational layer generates real-time 4D spacetime safety envelopes around human occupants and autonomous maintenance units. Before any internal repair action, the KCSP verifies that harmonic interference between human activity (footfalls, exercise) and repair tool frequencies does not exceed the critical safety buffer ΔΩ_crit for the curing material. If the constraint is violated, the repair is paused, rescheduled, or the human activity is gently redirected until the curing window closes. Pre-repair environmental pre-conditioning (increased air exchange, directional airflow) is automated.

KCSP Constraint Equation (Harmonic Interference Mitigation):

|ω_human(t) - ω_repair(t)| > ΔΩ_crit(M)

where ω_human is the dominant frequency spectrum of localized human activity, ω_repair is the resonant frequency of the maintenance tool, and ΔΩ_crit(M) is the material-dependent safety buffer for the curing matrix M. The KCSP engine enforces this in real time before and during every internal repair operation.

